    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 151739\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n939 AM PST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Calm and Cool for Sunday\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Saturday\n\nOur quick ridge pattern lasts through Sunday, keeping conditions \ncalm and seasonably cool with building high clouds. Temperatures\nfor the dessert valleys will mostly stick to the 60s and low \n70s, with Death Valley breaking into the upper 70s. Wilds will be \nmostly light with only a few localized breezes possible.\n\nThe next precip-maker arrives on Monday as a low pressure builds \ninto the Sierra Nevada that morning and spreads east through into \nthat night. This time of arrival is slightly slower than previous \nforecasts and shows that the feed of moisture has reduced \nslightly. While the moisture pull will still favor areas to the \neast of the Sierra, showers may be a little less consistent across\nthe region and may not filter into southern Nevada until well \nafter sunset. This slow line of showers will take its time to move\nacross the CWA, not arriving to Mohave Co until early Tuesday and\nexiting late that night. Expect around 0.02 to 0.20\" for all but \nNortheast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and Lincoln Co which will range \nfrom 0.30 to 0.60\", but remember this will be over a good amount \nof time. Wednesday will offer another pause in rain for most of \nthe region, but another batch of showers and snow arrives that \nevening to the Sierra. Showers will move into the rest of the \nregion into Thursday. This system looks to be on the colder side, \nleading to petter chances for snow across the higher peaks, but \nboth rain and snow accumulations look to be light. Precip chances \nfrom this system look to exit by Friday afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \neasterly winds become variable this evening, followed by westerly \nwinds overnight. Cloud cover gradually increases and cloud bases \nlower throughout the forecast period. FEW to SCT clouds with bases \nof 6-8kft move in overnight. Coverage increases to SCT to BKN at 4-\n7kft tomorrow morning. Showers may develop within the vicinity of \nthe terminal by tomorrow afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light winds with periods \nof variability can be expected for most locations throughout the TAF \nperiod. One exception is the northern Owens Valley including KBIH, \nwhere gusty southerly winds pick up late this morning, lasting into \nthe evening. Peak gusts should be in the 20 to 25 knot range. Cloud \ncover will increase and cloud bases will lower, with bases between 7 \nand 10kft by tomorrow morning. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
